= The Hamburg Uprising

On the morning of 23 October, armed Communists in Hamburg storm police stations in Barmbek, Schiffbek and other districts. The rest of Germany stays quiet. Whether the courier calling off the rising failed to arrive in time, or the Hamburg leaders chose to go ahead, is still argued over by historians.

Word can still reach the fighters.

- @let_fight
- @stop

@let_fight
title: Let Hamburg fight. Someone must show that the party meant it.
subtitle: Pleases <span class="f-stalin">Thälmann</span>, the <span class="f-left">Left</span> and <span class="f-ultra">Ultra-Left</span>. Angers the <span class="f-right">Right</span>. Costs lives and members.
on-arrival: {!
window.RF.lean(Q, {stalin: 5, left: 2, ultra: 2, right: -3});
Q.repression += 15;
Q.members -= 12;
Q.readiness -= 5;
Q.spd_relation -= 5;
!}

For three days a few hundred fighters hold out against the police and naval units. Dozens are killed on both sides. The party will make it a legend, and Thälmann's name will be tied to it for the rest of his life, though his actual part in it is unclear.

@stop
title: Send word to break off and save the cadres.
subtitle: Pleases the <span class="f-right">Right</span> and <span class="f-center">Middle Group</span>. Angers <span class="f-stalin">Thälmann</span> and the <span class="f-left">Left</span>.
on-arrival: {!
window.RF.lean(Q, {right: 2, center: 2, stalin: -4, left: -3});
Q.repression += 6;
Q.members -= 4;
Q.readiness += 2;
!}

The fighters withdraw on the second day and hide their weapons. Fewer die. In Hamburg, some call it a betrayal.
